+      <section id="outlook-and-conclusions">
+        <h2>Outlook &amp; Conclusions</h2>
+        <p>
+          In this dataset, the 2010s are the highest-average decade (<code>$76.45</code>), while the 2000s display the highest decade-level coefficient of variation (CV ≈ <code>0.52</code>), reflecting elevated dispersion relative to its mean.
+          The 2020s (partial decade through <code>2026-03-01</code>) show a high-price regime with meaningful volatility (<code>std $18.80</code>) and include both a sharp 2020 drawdown and subsequent higher-price behavior.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          For analysts, the practical takeaway is to treat crude oil as a regime-switching series: long runs can be relatively stable, but tail events dominate risk.
+          Historical context should therefore emphasize (1) the range of plausible drawdowns over 1–12 month horizons, (2) how quickly prices can re-rate, and (3) the fact that mean levels are not stationary across decades.
+        </p>
+        <p class="small">
+          Data limitation note: the dataset ends at <code>2026-03-01</code>. Any inference about the remainder of 2026 or beyond is out of scope and intentionally not included.
+        </p>
